he whole place coming down.\n\nThe ruins of the Amazon basin were a puzzle built by people who didn't want visitors. a star-metal blade was the key — literally; it fit a slot in the great door, and the stone sighed open after a thousand years."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price of Gold', 'text': "The backup team wasn't loyal — just paid. When Riley Fox offered them the truth about the mercenary Voss's promises (empty) versus a share of the legitimate expedition rights (real), they lowered their weapons. Money talks. Truth talks louder.\n\nAt the caravan, they made their stand: outnumbered, outgunned, but holding the high ground and the moral ground, which as the pilot Anya said weakly, 'is worth at least two guns.'\n\nThe trek out was worse than the trek in: the mercenary Voss's backup team, the river rising, the pilot Anya feverish from a scratch that turned ugly. Riley Fox carried the pilot Anya and a star-metal blade in turns, and learned what shoulders are for."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Real Treasure', 'text': "the mercenary Voss got fifteen years and a memoir deal. Riley Fox got the kid's wide eyes and the pilot Anya's friendship and a new map already arriving by courier. No return address. Of course.\n\nA kid asked Riley Fox if it was worth it — the danger, the lost years. Riley Fox thought of the river, the ruins, the pilot Anya's hand in the rapids. 'The thing,' Riley Fox said, 'was never the thing.'\n\nThey stood before a star-metal blade one last time. It had started all this: the legend, the race, the ruin, the rescue. Riley Fox saluted it. Some doors, once opened, stay open forever."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Next Horizon', 'text': "Somewhere, the mercenary Voss was reading about it in a cell and planning. Let them. The world is wide, and Riley Fox is faster, and the pilot Anya is smarter, and that's usually enough.\n\nRiley Fox took one last look at the Amazon basin, green and gold and dangerous. It had given them a star-metal blade, and taken nothing they weren't willing to give. Fair trade.\n\nThe plane — or boat, or truck, or camel — was already arranged. It always is, when a star-metal blade calls. That's the thing about legends: they don't end. They just wait for the next believer."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

