ook it and ran, because Riley Fox is not an idiot. The chase through the collapsing ruins was the kind of thing they'd tell for years: swinging, sliding, the tracker Ash with the rope, a jade compass in the bag, the whole place coming down."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price of Gold', 'text': "At the well, they made their stand: outnumbered, outgunned, but holding the high ground and the moral ground, which as the tracker Ash said weakly, 'is worth at least two guns.'\n\nthe warlord's men, bound and furious, offered everything: money, power, a share. Riley Fox listened politely and said no politely, which infuriated the warlord's men more than any insult. Some people can't be bought. Some treasures can't be sold.\n\nthe warlord's men was delivered to the authorities with the evidence. a jade compass was delivered to the museum, on loan, with Riley Fox's name on the plaque and the tracker Ash's name right beside it, where it belonged."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Real Treasure', 'text': "the warlord's men got fifteen years and a memoir deal. Riley Fox got the kid's wide eyes and the tracker Ash's friendship and a new map already arriving by courier. No return address. Of course.\n\ncuriosity is courage — Riley Fox wrote it in the expedition log, under the date, under the coordinates of the Coral Triangle. Then closed the book. Then opened the new map.\n\nThe museum opening was black tie. Riley Fox wore boots. the tracker Ash, recovered and insufferable, wore a grin. a jade compass sat behind glass, humming faintly, home at last among people who'd care for it."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Next Horizon', 'text': "Engines up. curiosity is courage. The horizon, as always, was just the beginning.\n\nthe tracker Ash was packed before Riley Fox finished reading the note. 'Where to?' the tracker Ash asked. Riley Fox pointed at the map's edge, past the Coral Triangle, past everything known. 'There,' Riley Fox said.\n\nRiley Fox took one last look at the Coral Triangle, green and gold and 